# GeoFrame deck.gl map on real data

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:geoframe, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"},
  {:req, "~> 0.5"}
])
```

## Binary maps with deck.gl

`GeoFrame.Kino.deck/2` ships geometry to the browser as typed-array buffers (no GeoJSON) and
draws it with deck.gl, so a million points stay interactive. deck.gl and the Carto basemap load
from the internet in your browser.

The examples use [Natural Earth](https://www.naturalearthdata.com/) vector data (public domain),
downloaded on first run into the system temp directory.

```elixir
base = "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nvkelso/natural-earth-vector/master/geojson"

fetch = fn name ->
  path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), name)

  unless File.exists?(path) do
    Req.get!("#{base}/#{name}", into: File.stream!(path)) |> then(& &1.status) |> then(&(200 = &1))
  end

  path
end
```

## World countries

Read the 110m countries file and color each country by population with the viridis ramp. Hover
for the name, population and GDP.

```elixir
countries =
  fetch.("ne_110m_admin_0_countries.geojson")
  |> GeoFrame.read_file!()
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(["NAME", "ADM0_A3", "CONTINENT", "POP_EST", "GDP_MD"])

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(countries,
  color: {"POP_EST", :viridis},
  opacity: 0.8,
  tooltip: ["NAME", "POP_EST", "GDP_MD"]
)
```

Population alone mostly maps country size. Project to an equal-area CRS (EPSG:6933), compute the
area in km² and color by people per km² instead; the widget reprojects back to longitude and
latitude by itself.

```elixir
density =
  countries
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.to_crs("EPSG:6933")
  |> then(fn gdf ->
    area_km2 = gdf |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.area() |> Explorer.Series.divide(1_000_000)
    GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put(gdf, "AREA_KM2", area_km2)
  end)
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.mutate_with(fn df ->
    [DENSITY: Explorer.Series.divide(df["POP_EST"], df["AREA_KM2"])]
  end)

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(density, color: {"DENSITY", :viridis}, tooltip: ["NAME", "POP_EST", "DENSITY"])
```

## Cities

The 10m populated places as points: radius by the largest population estimate, a fixed orange
color, and cities above one million people only. Hover for name, country and population.

```elixir
cities =
  fetch.("ne_10m_populated_places_simple.geojson")
  |> GeoFrame.read_file!()
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(["name", "adm0name", "pop_max", "megacity"])

big_cities = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter_with(cities, &Explorer.Series.greater(&1["pop_max"], 1_000_000))

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(big_cities,
  color: {230, 100, 30},
  radius: "pop_max",
  opacity: 0.7,
  tooltip: ["name", "adm0name", "pop_max"]
)
```

## Rivers

The 50m river centerlines are lines. Color them by the Natural Earth `scalerank` (smaller is a
more important river); `:width` also accepts a numeric column.

```elixir
rivers = fetch.("ne_50m_rivers_lake_centerlines.geojson") |> GeoFrame.read_file!()

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(rivers, color: {"scalerank", :viridis}, width: 2, tooltip: ["name", "scalerank"])
```

## Cities per country

A spatial join answers "which country is each city in?". Keep the country name and code, join
every city to the country polygon that contains it, then count the cities per country and
color the countries by that count.

```elixir
alias Explorer.DataFrame, as: DF

country_keys = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(countries, ["ADM0_A3"])

located = GeoFrame.sjoin(cities, country_keys, how: :inner, predicate: :within)

counts =
  located
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.data()
  |> DF.group_by("ADM0_A3")
  |> DF.summarise_with(&[N_CITIES: Explorer.Series.count(&1["name"])])

by_country = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.join(countries, counts, on: "ADM0_A3", how: :left)

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(by_country, color: {"N_CITIES", :viridis}, tooltip: ["NAME", "N_CITIES"])
```



## Smart Cells

With `geoframe` installed, Livebook's **Smart** menu offers two cells that write the code for
you:

* **Read spatial file** reads a file with `GeoFrame.read_file!/2`. Choose the path, the driver
  (or leave it on `auto`), a GeoPackage layer, an optional bounding box and target CRS, and
  tick **Show map** to end the cell with `GeoFrame.Kino.deck/2`.
* **Spatial join** lists the `GeoDataFrame` variables of the notebook (evaluate the cell that
  creates them first) and generates `GeoFrame.sjoin/3` or `GeoFrame.sjoin_nearest/3`.

The cells generate plain Elixir, which **Convert to Code cell** keeps. Point **Read spatial
file** at the cities file the notebook downloaded (`Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "ne_10m_populated_places_simple.geojson")`),
and in **Spatial join** pick `cities` on the left and `countries` on the right with the
`within` predicate. This is what they write, and it runs headlessly:

```elixir
path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "ne_10m_populated_places_simple.geojson")

places = GeoFrame.read_file!(path)
joined = GeoFrame.sjoin(places, countries, how: :inner, predicate: :within)

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(joined, radius: 3, tooltip: ["name", "NAME"])
```

## Scaling: one million points (optional)

Random points over South Florida, reprojected from Web Mercator to show that the widget
converts to longitude/latitude first. Color by a categorical column and size by a number.

```elixir
n = 1_000_000
lon = for _ <- 1..n, do: -80.9 + :rand.uniform() * 1.1
lat = for _ <- 1..n, do: 25.2 + :rand.uniform() * 1.0

points =
  GeoFrame.GeoSeries.from_xy(lon, lat, crs: "EPSG:4326")
  |> GeoFrame.GeoSeries.to_crs(3857)

frame =
  GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.new(
    Explorer.DataFrame.new(
      zone: Enum.map(1..n, &Enum.at(["a", "b", "c"], rem(&1, 3))),
      size: Enum.map(1..n, fn _ -> :rand.uniform() end)
    ),
    points
  )

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(frame,
  color: {:zone, :categorical},
  radius: :size,
  opacity: 0.6,
  tooltip: [:zone, :size]
)
```
